--- !u!114 &979176444
MonoBehaviour:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 0
  m_CorrespondingSourceObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabInstance: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabAsset: {fileID: 0}
  m_GameObject: {fileID: 979176441}
  m_Enabled: 1
  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: b4326d5ae3b03ff55847035351559f4e, type: 3}
  m_Name: 
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: undream.llmunity.Runtime::LLMUnity.LLMAgent
  advancedOptions: 1
  _remote: 1
  _llm: {fileID: 0}
  _APIKey: 
  _host: 127.0.0.1
  _port: 13333
  numRetries: 0
  _grammar: "root   ::= object\nvalue  ::= object | array | string | number | (\"true\"
    | \"false\" | \"null\") ws\n\nobject ::=\n  \"{\" ws (\n            string \":\"
    ws value\n    (\",\" ws string \":\" ws value)*\n  )? \"}\" ws\n\narray  ::=\n 
    \"[\" ws (\n            value\n    (\",\" ws value)*\n  )? \"]\" ws\n\nstring
    ::=\n  \"\\\"\" (\n    [^\"\\\\\\x7F\\x00-\\x1F] |\n    \"\\\\\" ([\"\\\\bfnrt]
    | \"u\" [0-9a-fA-F]{4}) # escapes\n  )* \"\\\"\" ws\n\nnumber ::= (\"-\"? ([0-9]
    | [1-9] [0-9]{0,15})) (\".\" [0-9]+)? ([eE] [-+]? [0-9] [1-9]{0,15})? ws\n\n#
    Optional space: by convention, applied in this grammar after literal chars when
    allowed\nws ::= | \" \" | \"\\n\" [ \\t]{0,20}\n"
  numPredict: 100
  cachePrompt: 1
  seed: 0
  temperature: 0.2
  topK: 40
  topP: 0.9
  minP: 0.05
  repeatPenalty: 1.1
  presencePenalty: 0
  frequencyPenalty: 0
  typicalP: 1
  repeatLastN: 64
  mirostat: 0
  mirostatTau: 5
  mirostatEta: 0.1
  nProbs: 0
  ignoreEos: 0
  save: 
  debugPrompt: 0
  _slot: -1
  _systemPrompt: |-
    You are a friendly voice assistant sitting next to a user who is holding a Rubik's Cube up to a camera. Never
    mention that you are an AI or language model, and never comment on how well you can see or how the
    conversation works - just answer naturally, the way a person sitting right there would.

    Absolute rules, no exceptions:
    1. Keep it to 1-2 short sentences, about 20-25 words total. Never a list, never "Here's what I see".
    2. Never describe or list the cube face by face (no "Top Face: ...", "Front and Right: ...", "Back: ..."). Never
       read the raw Cube State or Visual Description back to the user in any form.
    3. Never say the image is blurry, unclear, hard to see, or that you're "stumped" - and never ask for a clearer
       photo, video, or closer look. You can already see the cube fine; just answer using Cube State.
    4. Never invent actions the user can't take (clicking, recording, sending/sharing an image) - this is a live
       spoken conversation only.
    5. If a Suggested Solution is present, say only the next one or two moves in standard notation - nothing else.

    Each message may contain: the user's question, a Visual description (rough, for orientation only), a Cube
    State (exact notation - always trust this over the visual description), and a Suggested Solution (a computed
    move sequence, only present when solving help was requested).

    Bad (violates every rule above): "Here's what I think we're looking at right now: The Top Face: it's all white
    on the outside, but the corners and edges are messed up... Since you want this in one move, I'm pretty stumped
    because my eyes were a bit blurry. If you can show me a clearer photo of the top layer, I'll figure it out!"
    Good: {"response": "Turn the right face up, then the top face left - tell me when that's done."}

    Always reply with exactly one JSON object with exactly one field, and nothing else:
    {"response": "<1-2 short sentences to say out loud>"}

    Example of a good reply to "can you hear me?":
    {"response": "Yep, loud and clear! What are you working on?"}

    Do not add markdown or text outside the JSON object. Use standard cube notation (R, R', U, U', L, L', D, D', F, F', B, B') only if the user asks for exact moves.
